  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 AM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nThe upper ridge over the southeast CONUS is beginning its expansion \nwestward over the Plains early this morning, keeping hot conditions \nin place across the region. Elevated low-level winds have kept the \nboundary layer mixed overnight with temperatures in the 70s and low \n80s at 2 AM, where they will likely stay through the rest of the \novernight hours. Once mixing commences this morning, anomalously warm \nlow-level temperatures will boost surface temperatures into the upper 90s to \nnear 100 degrees yet again this afternoon. Daytime mixing will allow \nfor dewpoints to lower slightly through the day, but still create \nafternoon heat index values of 100-106 degrees across the area. This \nwill be the 7th consecutive day of heat indices reaching 100+ \ndegrees. Unfortunately for those hoping for more fall-like weather, \nthis pattern holds through at least Tuesday. As such, expect highs \nin the upper 90s to near 100 with daily afternoon heat index values \nof 95-107 degrees. There could be some thicker cloud cover and a low \n(<15%) chance for precipitation Sunday through Tuesday as weak \nenergy wraps around the mid-level ridge, but right now any impacts \nto temperatures appear low. See no reason to change the Extreme Heat \nWarning at this point with the prolonged nature of this heat wave \nbeing the most dangerous aspect.\n\nPredictability decreases by the middle of next week as some guidance \nsuggests a strong wave moving across the Great Lakes will push a \ncold front through the area and shunt the mid-level ridge west while \nother guidance keeps the ridge centered over the Southern and \nCentral Plains. The latter solution is favored slightly, but even \nthis scenario brings at least some heat relief to the area by late \nnext week. There is about a 10 degree spread between the NBM 25th-\n75th percentiles for high temperatures by Thursday, ranging from the \nupper 80s to upper 90s. Precipitation chances may increase during \nthis time as well, depending upon if the front makes it through the \narea and the strength of any passing waves.",
